# Think Session Strategy

Use separate Think sessions / agent instances for different Slack conversation contexts.

Default mapping:

```txt
DM with bot:
slack:{teamId}:dm:{userId}

Slack thread:
slack:{teamId}:channel:{channelId}:thread:{threadTs}

Channel-level memory:
slack:{teamId}:channel:{channelId}

Workspace-level memory:
slack:{teamId}:workspace
```

Prefer thread-level sessions for channel conversations.

Do not use the entire Slack channel as one active chat session by default because the context will become noisy.

Recommended behavior:

```txt
DM with bot       → separate Think session / agent instance
Slack thread      → separate Think session / agent instance
Channel memory    → shared memory / summary / knowledge storage
Workspace memory  → optional shared memory / knowledge storage
```

Use a dedicated resolver:

```txt
src/modules/slack/slack-session-resolver.ts
```

Example:

```ts
export function resolveSlackSessionId(input: {
  teamId: string;
  channelId: string;
  userId: string;
  channelType?: string;
  threadTs?: string;
  messageTs: string;
}): string {
  if (input.channelType === "im") {
    return `slack:${input.teamId}:dm:${input.userId}`;
  }

  const threadId = input.threadTs ?? input.messageTs;

  return `slack:${input.teamId}:channel:${input.channelId}:thread:${threadId}`;
}
```

Keep session resolving deterministic and easy to test.

