# Slack Listener Rules

The Slack listener must be thin.

It may use Node.js APIs and Slack SDKs because it runs outside Cloudflare Workers.

The Slack listener should:

* connect to Slack Socket Mode
* receive events
* acknowledge Slack events quickly
* normalize event shape
* add idempotency metadata
* send the event to the Worker
* log operational errors
* retry Worker calls safely

It should not:

* execute Think agent logic
* call `@cloudflare/think`
* call Workers AI
* call Cloudflare storage
* contain domain/business logic
* store conversation memory
* perform RAG

The listener should call the Worker with an internal auth token or request signature.

Never expose the Worker internal token.

Never commit `.env` files.

---

# Slack Event Idempotency

Slack may retry events.

Every event sent from the Slack listener to the Worker must include an idempotency key.

Use this priority:

```txt
event_id
client_msg_id
event_ts
message_ts
```

The Worker must handle duplicate events safely.

Do not process the same Slack event multiple times.

Preferred normalized event type:

```ts
export type NormalizedSlackMessageEvent = {
  teamId: string;
  channelId: string;
  userId: string;
  text: string;
  messageTs: string;
  threadTs?: string;
  eventId?: string;
  eventTs?: string;
  clientMsgId?: string;
  channelType?: "im" | "channel" | "group" | "mpim" | string;
  idempotencyKey: string;
};
```

Do not rely on raw Slack payloads in use cases.

Normalize Slack events at the boundary.

