# Storage Rules

Do not assume that all storage must be Cloudflare-native.

The project may use:

* Think / Durable Object session storage
* Durable Objects storage
* Cloudflare D1
* Cloudflare KV
* Cloudflare R2
* Cloudflare Vectorize
* Cloudflare Queues
* Cloudflare experimental storage APIs
* external databases
* custom storage services
* in-memory development storage
* third-party vector databases
* third-party object storage

All storage access must go through ports/interfaces.

Use cases must not know whether the implementation uses Cloudflare storage, experimental storage, external storage, Think storage, or mock storage.

Suggested storage mapping:

```txt
Active conversation session state → @cloudflare/think
Agent durable runtime state       → Durable Objects / Think
Relational app data               → D1 / external SQL / experimental relational storage
Lightweight cache                 → KV / external cache / experimental cache storage
Files and attachments             → R2 / external object storage / experimental file storage
Semantic search                   → Vectorize / external vector database / custom vector adapter
Background jobs                   → Queues / external queue
Development tests                 → in-memory adapters
```

Good:

```ts
export interface MemoryPort {
  getConversationSummary(conversationId: string): Promise<string | null>;
  saveConversationSummary(conversationId: string, summary: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

Bad inside a use case:

```ts
await env.DB.prepare("SELECT * FROM messages").all();
```

Storage implementations should live in adapters:

```txt
src/adapters/storage/
  d1-storage.adapter.ts
  kv-storage.adapter.ts
  r2-file-storage.adapter.ts
  experimental-storage.adapter.ts
  external-postgres-storage.adapter.ts
  memory-storage.adapter.ts
```

Experimental Cloudflare storage may be used, but only behind ports.

Do not couple business logic to experimental APIs.

